 * @param {string|Object} queryOrParams - The search query string or params object
 * @returns {Promise<Object>} - Search results
 */
const search = async (queryOrParams = {}) => {
  // If it's a string, wrap it in {query: string}
  // Otherwise, use the object as-is
  const payload = typeof queryOrParams === 'string' 
    ? { query: queryOrParams }
    : queryOrParams;

  return client.post('/search', payload).then(res => res.data)
}
```

With this change, your search function will work correctly in both scenarios:
1. When you call `search('')` for pages, it will send `{ query: '' }`
2. When you call `search({ filter: {...} })` for databases, it will send `{ filter: {...} }`

This update preserves backward compatibility with existing code while also supporting the filter-based search for databases.

