content.text:
The issue is that you're trying to delete a page using the blocks endpoint, but Notion's API requires you to use the pages endpoint for pages.

Looking at your notion-api.js file (document #10), I can see you have two different functions:

1. `deleteBlock()` - This attempts to archive a block through the blocks endpoint
2. `updatePage()` - This can update a page's properties, including its archived state

The error message is very clear about what to do: "Updating a page via the blocks endpoint unsupported. Call patch /v1/pages/:page_id instead"

The solution is to replace the call to `notionApi.deleteBlock(page.id)` with `notionApi.updatePage(page.id, {}, true)` in your delete route handler. The third parameter `true` in your updatePage function sets the archived flag.

Looking at your updatePage function in notion-api.js:

```javascript
const updatePage = async (page_id, properties = {}, archived = false) => {
  const payload = { properties }

  if (archived) {
    payload.archived = true
  }

  return client.patch(`/pages/${page_id}`, payload).then(res => res.data)
}
```

This is the correct function to use for archiving pages. It properly sends the request to the pages endpoint instead of the blocks endpoint.

With this change, your delete route should work correctly without any further issues. The pattern here is consistent with the other fixes - it's about using the right API endpoint for each Notion object type.

