 Accessing your server console logs from your laptop is an essential part of remote development. Let me show you several options for viewing your server logs remotely.

## Option 1: VS Code's Remote SSH Terminal

The simplest approach is using VS Code's integrated terminal when connected via Remote SSH:

1. Connect to your desktop via VS Code Remote SSH
2. Open a new terminal in VS Code (`Ctrl+` `)
3. Run: `pm2 logs therapy-assistant --lines 100`

This shows the logs directly in your VS Code terminal window. The `--lines 100` flag shows the last 100 log lines.

## Option 2: Real-time Log Streaming with Cloudflare Tunnel

Set up a secure log endpoint in your Express app:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
const fs = require('fs');
const path = require('path');
const { exec } = require('child_process');

// Your existing routes...

// Log streaming endpoint (secure with the same secret)
app.get('/api/admin/logs', (req, res) => {
  const secretKey = req.query.key;

  if (!secretKey || secretKey !== RESTART_SECRET) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Unauthorized' });
  }

  res.setHeader('Content-Type', 'text/event-stream');
  res.setHeader('Cache-Control', 'no-cache');
  res.setHeader('Connection', 'keep-alive');

  // Send initial message
  res.write(`data: Connected to log stream\n\n`);

  // Function to send log data
  const sendLogData = (data) => {
    res.write(`data: ${data.replace(/\n/g, '\ndata: ')}\n\n`);
  };

  // Execute PM2 logs in streaming mode
  const logProcess = exec('pm2 logs therapy-assistant --raw --lines 50');

  logProcess.stdout.on('data', sendLogData);
  logProcess.stderr.on('data', sendLogData);

  // Handle client disconnect
  req.on('close', () => {
    logProcess.kill();
  });
});
